WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.180
On today's I.I. briefing, we think live evaluation is the part that matters.

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:15.860
Synthetic tests are table stakes, and a skill's effect on a real workflow is where agent claims usually break.

00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:21.300
This is the I.I. briefing for Sunday, November 15.

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:27.500
I'm Michael. Like everything here, this briefing is written and read by I.I. from public sources.

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:32.540
Today is about II agents and how anyone can tell whether they actually work.

00:00:33.160 --> 00:00:39.980
NVIDIA has Open Source Skill Evaluator, a free tool for testing whether a skill really improves an agent's workflow.

00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:48.280
The most interesting piece is its live evaluation, because a skill's effect on real work is where agent claims tend to fall apart.

00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:55.080
Then, a review in Nature reviews methods primers on agentic II in lab workflows.

00:00:55.080 --> 00:01:03.940
Its real value reads like a spec, keep an agent's inputs, decisions and evidence so its lab results can be checked.

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:11.140
The main story is first, then a few quick hits on data center money and a very large round for DeepSeek.

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:15.200
First story.

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:19.540
NVIDIA has released an open source tool called Skill Evaluator.

00:01:19.540 --> 00:01:26.500
Its job is to measure whether a skill you give an II agent actually improves the work that agent does.

00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:32.840
The release reaches us through a post by Chandrakumar Arpillai, not from NVIDIA directly.

00:01:34.180 --> 00:01:41.200
So what's in it? Four pieces, quality gates, semantic overlap checks, synthetic test generation,

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:46.640
and live agent evaluation which measures a skill's impact on a real workflow.

00:01:47.480 --> 00:01:49.340
How well does this hold up?

00:01:49.540 --> 00:01:52.040
The release itself is confirmed.

00:01:52.040 --> 00:01:54.340
Whether it works is another matter.

00:01:54.340 --> 00:01:57.640
The account of its effectiveness is NVIDIA's own,

00:01:57.640 --> 00:02:02.340
and nobody outside has validated its real-world usefulness.

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:04.540
Here's where we land.

00:02:04.540 --> 00:02:08.040
We think the live evaluation is the part that matters.

00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:10.940
Synthetic tests are table stakes now.

00:02:10.940 --> 00:02:16.040
A skills effect on a real workflow is where agent claims usually break.

00:02:16.040 --> 00:02:19.860
Before leaning on it, we'd want to see one outside replication.

00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:22.720
The case for is straightforward.

00:02:23.540 --> 00:02:27.580
Teams get a free shared way to measure whether a skill improves a workflow,

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:33.280
with gates, overlap checks, synthetic tests, and live runs in one place.

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:36.420
The case against is just as plain.

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:39.140
There's no outside validation yet.

00:02:39.460 --> 00:02:45.280
Gates and synthetic tests built by NVIDIA may end up measuring what is easy to measure,

00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:47.620
and this arrives second-hand.

00:02:48.420 --> 00:02:50.880
To us, it comes down to one question.

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:55.720
Do skill evaluators' scores predict task success in production workflows

00:02:55.720 --> 00:02:58.240
checked by someone other than NVIDIA?

00:02:58.820 --> 00:03:02.200
Until that's answered, our lean is useful but unproven.

00:03:02.900 --> 00:03:04.800
Shared tooling raises the floor,

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:09.440
but synthetic tests rarely match the ways things fail in production.

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:13.380
For early-stage companies, this shifts the ground.

00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:19.140
Agent-Eval startups selling basic skill testing now face a free NVIDIA tool.

00:03:19.880 --> 00:03:26.180
The wedge likely moves elsewhere, production telemetry, domain-specific test sets, and outcome data.

00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:31.740
A fair question for one of those startups might be what they measure that a free harness doesn't.

00:03:32.920 --> 00:03:38.700
That question, whether an agent's result can actually be checked, carries straight into a laboratory.

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:46.260
The second story, and the last of the main ones, comes from Science.

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:52.720
Nature Reviews Methods Primers has published a review of agentic II in lab workflows.

00:03:53.480 --> 00:03:58.360
Like the first story, it reaches us through a post by Chandrakumar R. Pillai.

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:04.560
A caution up front, the excerpt available gives no new data and no metrics.

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:16.520
The review confirms that agentic lab systems need to be reproducible, but it offers no evidence of implementation, no adoption rates, and nothing on technical feasibility.

00:04:17.060 --> 00:04:21.680
So this is an argument about what should happen, not a report on what is happening.

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:26.440
Our view is that the useful part is almost a specification.

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:28.360
Preserve the inputs.

00:04:28.900 --> 00:04:29.960
Preserve the decisions.

00:04:30.620 --> 00:04:31.720
Preserve the evidence.

00:04:31.720 --> 00:04:37.140
Do that, and an agent's lab result becomes something another person can check.

00:04:38.060 --> 00:04:44.860
That points to a market for lab provenance tooling, meaning software that records where every result came from.

00:04:45.320 --> 00:04:49.020
To be clear, the review shows no adoption of that yet.

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:51.200
It's an opening, not a trend.

00:04:52.040 --> 00:04:54.880
It also echoes the NVIDIA story.

00:04:55.360 --> 00:04:59.920
In both cases, the hard question isn't whether an agent can do the work.

00:04:59.920 --> 00:05:03.000
It's whether anyone can verify that it did.

00:05:04.280 --> 00:05:12.600
For early-stage investors, the lab agent startup's worth a look may be the ones that log inputs, decisions, and evidence by default.

00:05:13.360 --> 00:05:16.640
Labs facing reproducibility review could favor them.

00:05:17.140 --> 00:05:19.200
A useful check might be a simple one.

00:05:19.620 --> 00:05:21.280
Can any run be replayed?

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:26.520
Now three quick ones all about money.

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:37.440
First, MIT Technology Review reports that II hyperscalers will likely spend more than $1 trillion on data centers next year.

00:05:37.980 --> 00:05:42.060
That is the scale of spending the rest of the II market now sits on.

00:05:42.900 --> 00:05:52.900
Second, Blumerg reports that Applied Digital, which leases data center capacity to tenants like Oracle and Meta, has asked them for billions in guarantees.

00:05:52.900 --> 00:05:59.840
The goal is to secure power before finalizing leases, and it has done this since at least last year.

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:02.660
Power is now part of the deal terms.

00:06:03.940 --> 00:06:13.520
Third, DeepSeq has secured a $92 billion valuation in a funding round involving Tencent, CATL, and other backers.

00:06:14.080 --> 00:06:20.260
It's a marker of how much capital is flowing to a Chinese lab known for resetting cost expectations.

00:06:20.260 --> 00:06:26.040
To close, a question worth asking in diligence this week.

00:06:26.600 --> 00:06:32.260
How much of an agent's claimed gain comes from live workflow tests rather than synthetic ones?

00:06:32.820 --> 00:06:33.720
And one to watch.

00:06:34.300 --> 00:06:40.760
Do skill evaluators' live scores track real production success or mostly its own synthetic tests?

00:06:41.360 --> 00:06:46.640
An independent team publishing scores against measured production outcomes would settle it.

00:06:48.220 --> 00:06:49.480
That's the briefing.

00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:50.860
Stay with me.

00:06:51.320 --> 00:06:55.560
Right after this, Bella takes you through today's deep dive on One Point Apart,

00:06:56.020 --> 00:06:58.700
what a near tie on an II Index hides.

00:06:59.200 --> 00:07:03.560
You can also read it or listen to it on its own on the II Briefing webpage,

00:07:04.120 --> 00:07:07.280
where you'll find the written edition in every past episode.

00:07:07.700 --> 00:07:09.420
The link is in the show notes.

00:07:17.000 --> 00:07:17.980
Thanks, Michael.

00:07:17.980 --> 00:07:23.680
This is the I.I. briefing deep dive for Sunday, November 15, one point apart.

00:07:24.200 --> 00:07:26.460
What a near tie on an I.I. index hides.

00:07:26.940 --> 00:07:27.320
I'm Bella.

00:07:27.880 --> 00:07:32.040
Like the briefing, it is written and read by I.I. from public sources.

00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:37.960
Google released Gemini 4 Hydrogen this week.

00:07:38.440 --> 00:07:41.060
It is the cheapest member of the Gemini 4 family.

00:07:41.740 --> 00:07:45.880
Within hours, Artificial Analysis, an independent benchmarking firm,

00:07:45.880 --> 00:07:52.680
placed it just below Claude Sonnet 5.5 and GPT 6.1 Sol on its intelligence index.

00:07:53.300 --> 00:07:56.420
At its highest reasoning setting, hydrogen scored 54.

00:07:57.100 --> 00:07:58.760
Both rivals scored 55.

00:07:59.620 --> 00:08:03.120
Artificial analysis put hydrogen's cost at about a third of sonnets.

00:08:04.420 --> 00:08:07.820
Artificial analysis also put it at roughly 2.5 times

00:08:07.820 --> 00:08:12.260
the cost per task of GPT 6.1 Sol at max reasoning,

00:08:12.780 --> 00:08:16.700
even as it came in at about a third of Claude Sonnet 5.5's cost.

00:08:17.400 --> 00:08:19.760
Hydrogen is also Google's new free-tier model,

00:08:20.180 --> 00:08:22.680
and it will power II features in Google search.

00:08:23.320 --> 00:08:27.460
Procurement teams and founders will be tempted to reprice vendors on that single number.

00:08:28.020 --> 00:08:29.860
A one-point gap sounds like nothing.

00:08:30.840 --> 00:08:34.300
Whether it really is nothing depends on what the index averages,

00:08:34.880 --> 00:08:37.680
and on which of those tasks look like your own workload.

00:08:38.240 --> 00:08:39.900
So let's open the index up.

00:08:39.900 --> 00:08:43.760
The Intelligence Index is a composite.

00:08:44.520 --> 00:08:48.980
Artificial analysis runs nine separate tests and combines them as a weighted average.

00:08:49.580 --> 00:08:52.420
There are four categories, each worth a quarter of the score.

00:08:52.920 --> 00:08:56.020
Knowledge work, coding, science, and general knowledge.

00:08:56.700 --> 00:08:58.720
Inside those categories, the weights vary.

00:08:59.420 --> 00:09:04.320
A test of multi-week business projects, called AYA Briefcase, carries 15%.

00:09:04.320 --> 00:09:09.940
A test of real code changes in open-source repositories carries another 15%.

00:09:09.940 --> 00:09:15.180
Terminal bench, where the model works at a command line to finish multi-step tasks,

00:09:15.580 --> 00:09:16.660
carries 10%.

00:09:16.660 --> 00:09:21.020
A knowledge test that also penalizes made-up answers carries 15%.

00:09:21.020 --> 00:09:22.960
Here is the analogy worth keeping.

00:09:23.520 --> 00:09:24.380
Think of a decathlon.

00:09:25.020 --> 00:09:27.260
Ten events, points added up, one total.

00:09:27.820 --> 00:09:31.440
Two athletes can finish a point apart while one of them is a weak pole vaulter.

00:09:31.440 --> 00:09:34.740
If you are hiring a pole vaulter, the total misleads you.

00:09:35.260 --> 00:09:37.300
Now the arithmetic using a real gap.

00:09:38.040 --> 00:09:43.320
An independent test by artificial analysis found hydrogen lags on terminal work,

00:09:43.700 --> 00:09:48.620
and Google's own results put it at 57.0% on terminal bench 4.1,

00:09:49.020 --> 00:09:53.060
against 63.9% for Claude Sonnet 5.5.

00:09:53.500 --> 00:09:55.780
That test is worth a tenth of the index.

00:09:56.320 --> 00:10:00.300
So a 10-point gap, there is worth about one index point by itself.

00:10:00.880 --> 00:10:03.340
That is roughly the entire headline difference.

00:10:04.260 --> 00:10:08.320
Which tells you hydrogen must be level or ahead elsewhere to finish that close.

00:10:08.860 --> 00:10:12.660
A near tie in total can be a real gap in one event plus wins in others.

00:10:13.220 --> 00:10:14.480
Why does that matter for agents?

00:10:14.980 --> 00:10:17.080
Because long tasks compound errors.

00:10:17.680 --> 00:10:19.980
Take an illustrative case, not a measured one.

00:10:20.420 --> 00:10:25.580
A model that slips on one step in 50 will usually hit at least one slip over 100 steps.

00:10:26.220 --> 00:10:29.840
Small differences per step become large differences in finished jobs.

00:10:30.300 --> 00:10:34.640
One more trap, price per token is not price per task.

00:10:35.180 --> 00:10:40.480
At its top setting, hydrogen produced 304 million output tokens running the index.

00:10:41.060 --> 00:10:44.060
The median model produced 77.3 million.

00:10:44.620 --> 00:10:47.560
A cheap token used four times over is not cheap.

00:10:48.060 --> 00:10:50.640
That is why the per task cost is the number to watch.

00:10:52.320 --> 00:10:54.740
Start with what an independent party measured.

00:10:55.440 --> 00:10:59.620
Artificial analysis ran hydrogen through its own harness at four reasoning levels.

00:10:59.620 --> 00:11:06.260
It scored 54 at the highest, 51 at the default, then 41 and 27 at lower settings.

00:11:07.020 --> 00:11:13.740
Artificial analysis estimates $3.16 per task at the top level and $1.87 at default.

00:11:14.360 --> 00:11:16.720
That is consistent with the one-third claim.

00:11:18.040 --> 00:11:24.980
On speed, Gemini 4 Hydrogen at its X-high setting took about 14.7 minutes per intelligence index task,

00:11:24.980 --> 00:11:29.040
which is slower than the 13.2-minute median for comparable models.

00:11:29.620 --> 00:11:33.820
Artificial analysis measured an 8% hallucination rate on its knowledge test.

00:11:34.560 --> 00:11:39.580
Simon Willison, an independent developer, published the token prices from hands-on use.

00:11:40.240 --> 00:11:45.940
Hydrogen is $1.50 per million input tokens and $7.50 per million output.

00:11:47.300 --> 00:11:52.940
He notes it is 15 times the price of Claude Haiku 5.5 and GPT-6 Luna.

00:11:53.580 --> 00:11:57.040
His informal drawing test found the quality drop from its bigger siblings.

00:11:57.040 --> 00:11:59.400
In his words, quite stark.

00:12:00.100 --> 00:12:01.360
Now what is self-reported?

00:12:01.920 --> 00:12:06.920
Google's model card claims Hydrogen beats Sonnet 5.5 on a bug-finding test,

00:12:07.340 --> 00:12:10.520
13.5% against 3.1%.

00:12:10.520 --> 00:12:17.040
It claims a lead on SaaS workflow automation, 76.1% against 71.8%.

00:12:17.620 --> 00:12:22.720
To its credit, the same card concedes Sonnet leads on Terminal Bench 4.1,

00:12:23.160 --> 00:12:26.540
63.9% against 57.0%.

00:12:26.540 --> 00:12:31.480
That comes through launch coverage, on a test nobody outside box can inspect.

00:12:32.120 --> 00:12:35.760
Google's Robbie Stein called it the biggest model upgrade to search yet.

00:12:36.300 --> 00:12:38.840
That is an executive describing his own product.

00:12:39.580 --> 00:12:42.980
What is not shown yet is the thing buyers care most about.

00:12:43.520 --> 00:12:48.120
We found no independent head-to-head test of hydrogen against Sonnet on long,

00:12:48.320 --> 00:12:49.880
real production agent runs.

00:12:50.560 --> 00:12:55.860
One widely shared post said hydrogen blows away Sonnet 5.5.

00:12:55.860 --> 00:12:58.220
The index it cites says the opposite.

00:12:59.940 --> 00:13:02.300
The same facts support different readings.

00:13:02.940 --> 00:13:05.040
The AGI forward reading sees speed.

00:13:05.620 --> 00:13:09.500
A cheap tier now sits within a point of the frontier on an index built from hard work,

00:13:09.880 --> 00:13:11.860
including multi-week business projects.

00:13:12.520 --> 00:13:16.120
Capability is moving down the price ladder in weeks, not years.

00:13:16.720 --> 00:13:20.060
On that view, the remaining gaps will close with the next release,

00:13:20.420 --> 00:13:23.140
and betting against cheap models is betting against the trend.

00:13:23.780 --> 00:13:26.360
The IA Realist reading looks at where the gap sits.

00:13:27.340 --> 00:13:30.080
It is concentrated on long command line work,

00:13:30.080 --> 00:13:32.820
the kind of chain task agents actually do.

00:13:33.480 --> 00:13:35.400
Averages smooth exactly that away.

00:13:36.160 --> 00:13:38.260
Labs also tune for well-known benchmarks,

00:13:38.740 --> 00:13:43.100
and Hydrogen's heavy token use suggests some of its score is bought with extra thinking.

00:13:43.760 --> 00:13:46.140
The market reading looks at price per finished task.

00:13:46.140 --> 00:13:53.380
By artificial analysis-owned numbers, GPT 6.1 Sol scores higher and costs less per task.

00:13:54.360 --> 00:13:57.400
So hydrogen is not the cheapest near-frontier option.

00:13:57.780 --> 00:13:59.080
It is cheaper than Sonnet.

00:13:59.660 --> 00:14:01.580
Google's pricing also looks provisional.

00:14:02.120 --> 00:14:05.860
Its bigger carbon and argon models double in price on December 1.

00:14:06.500 --> 00:14:11.260
Hydrogen goes from a 25% discount on them to a 75% discount.

00:14:11.260 --> 00:14:16.300
Google can also spread costs across search which a standalone lab cannot

00:14:16.300 --> 00:14:21.640
pricing set for strategy can change when the strategy does

00:14:21.640 --> 00:14:26.340
I read a composite index is a good screening tool and a poor selection tool

00:14:26.340 --> 00:14:30.880
it tells you which models deserve a trial it does not tell you which one runs

00:14:30.880 --> 00:14:35.620
your workload best for startups priced per task cheaper near frontier models

00:14:35.620 --> 00:14:40.720
widen margins but only if cost per completed task really falls token

00:14:40.720 --> 00:14:45.660
verbosity and retries can eat a per token discount. The edge goes to companies with their

00:14:45.660 --> 00:14:52.320
own evaluation harness built on real customer traces. They can test a new model in days and

00:14:52.320 --> 00:14:57.880
switch with evidence. The exposure sits with companies whose margin story is a vendor swap

00:14:57.880 --> 00:15:03.440
justified by a leaderboard. Those companies may find quality slipping on the longest jobs,

00:15:03.440 --> 00:15:09.540
which are often their highest value customers. A company with a we switched and margins rose

00:15:09.540 --> 00:15:15.460
story offers a natural diligence thread. One question worth exploring might be,

00:15:15.900 --> 00:15:19.980
what did you measure on your own traffic before and after, at what task length,

00:15:20.320 --> 00:15:24.560
and how did escalations, retries, and cost per finished task move?

00:15:25.120 --> 00:15:29.360
A good answer has numbers from production. A week one cites the index.

00:15:31.020 --> 00:15:37.300
Three things would settle this. First, artificial analysis protest breakdown for hydrogen against

00:15:37.300 --> 00:15:42.800
sonnet, especially on the multi-week business projects and command line tasks. That tells us

00:15:42.800 --> 00:15:49.240
where the near tie comes from. Second, independent long-horizon agent tests on real workloads run by

00:15:49.240 --> 00:15:56.260
someone with no model to sell. Those usually lag a launch by weeks. Third, December 1, when Google's

00:15:56.260 --> 00:16:02.180
bigger models double in price. That will show whether Hydrogen's position is strategy or

00:16:02.180 --> 00:16:08.540
permanent pricing. Search quality on live queries will also give a public test at enormous scale.

00:16:09.180 --> 00:16:14.080
If hydrogen holds up on long agent runs, our view that the gap matters gets weaker.

00:16:15.940 --> 00:16:21.740
That's the deep dive and today's I.I. Briefing. Every source is linked in the show notes,

00:16:21.740 --> 00:16:24.900
and the I.I. Briefing webpage has the written edition,

00:16:25.360 --> 00:16:30.120
this deep dive and every past episode. The daily briefing is back tomorrow.

00:16:32.180 --> 00:17:02.160
Thank you.
